module

public import TauCeti.LinearAlgebra.AffineSpace.AffineEquiv
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.RootSystem.WeylGroup

/-!
# Affine reflections and the affine Weyl group

A reflection of a root pairing fixes the linear hyperplane on which a coroot functional vanishes.
Translating that hyperplane to the level set `⟨x, αᵢ^∨⟩ = k` gives an affine involution of the
weight space,

`sᵢ,ₖ x = x - (⟨x, αᵢ^∨⟩ - k) αᵢ`,

which is `TauCeti.affineReflection`. The **affine Weyl group** is the subgroup of affine
automorphisms of the weight space generated by these involutions as `k` runs over the integers.
Composing two reflections in parallel walls is a translation by a multiple of a root, so the affine
Weyl group contains every translation by the root lattice `Q = ℤΦ`; conversely its linear parts are
products of linear reflections, and its translation parts stay in `Q`. The resulting description

`W_aff = Q ⋊ W`

is `TauCeti.mem_affineWeylGroup_iff`: an affine automorphism belongs to `W_aff` exactly when its
linear part lies in the Weyl group and its value at the origin lies in the root lattice. Read
through `AffineEquiv.linearHom` this is a split short exact sequence, with
`TauCeti.map_linearHom_affineWeylGroup` the surjection onto `W` and
`TauCeti.constVAdd_mem_affineWeylGroup_iff` its kernel.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.affineReflection`: the reflection of the weight space in an affine wall.
* `TauCeti.affineWeylGroup`: the affine Weyl group, generated by the affine reflections in the
  walls at integral levels.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.affineReflection_mul_affineReflection`: two reflections in parallel walls compose to the
  translation by the corresponding multiple of the root, so
  `TauCeti.constVAdd_root_eq_affineReflection_mul` writes the translation by a root as a product of
  two affine reflections.
* `TauCeti.constVAdd_mul_affineReflection_mul_constVAdd_inv` and
  `TauCeti.reflection_toAffineEquiv_mul_affineReflection_mul_inv`: conjugating an affine reflection
  by a translation shifts the level of its wall by the coroot value of the translation vector, and
  conjugating it by a linear reflection permutes the roots and keeps the level, so the affine Weyl
  group permutes the affine walls among themselves.
* `TauCeti.mem_affineWeylGroup_iff`: the semidirect-product description `W_aff = Q ⋊ W`, with
  `TauCeti.eq_constVAdd_mul_of_mem_affineWeylGroup` the factorisation it provides.
* `TauCeti.constVAdd_mem_affineWeylGroup_iff`: the translations in `W_aff` are exactly the
  translations by the root lattice.
* `TauCeti.map_linearHom_affineWeylGroup` and
  `TauCeti.map_linearHom_affineWeylGroup_eq_range_weightHom`: the linear parts of `W_aff` are
  exactly the Weyl group, in the two spellings Mathlib offers for it.
* `TauCeti.mem_rootSpan_of_mem_closure_range_reflection`: the Weyl group preserves the root
  lattice, which is what makes the product above a semidirect one. Mathlib's
  `RootPairing.rootSpan_mem_invtSubmodule_reflection` is the statement for a single reflection and
  for the span over the coefficient ring rather than over `ℤ`, which is not the lattice the
  translations of the affine Weyl group range over.
* `TauCeti.constVAdd_root_ne_toAffineEquiv`: the translation by a root is not the affine
  automorphism underlying any linear one, so the affine Weyl group of a pairing with a root is
  strictly larger than its Weyl group.

## Implementation notes

Everything is stated for the weight space `M` of an arbitrary root pairing `P`, so the affine
geometry of the *coweight* space, where the coroot lattice `Q^∨` translates and the fundamental
alcove lives, is the same construction applied to `P.flip`;
`TauCeti.mem_affineWeylGroup_flip_iff` records that instantiation.

The level of a wall is an element of the coefficient ring `R` rather than an integer, because an
affine reflection in a wall at an arbitrary level is what conjugation produces, and only the
integral walls are asked to generate the affine Weyl group. No finiteness, reducedness or
crystallographic hypothesis is needed for any statement here: the root lattice is the `ℤ`-span
`P.rootSpan ℤ` of the roots and the Weyl group is the group generated by the linear reflections,
whatever the coefficients.

The affine reflections and the affine Weyl group are new notions rather than additions to
Mathlib's root-pairing API, so they sit in the `TauCeti` namespace with the pairing as an explicit
argument, as `TauCeti.posRoots`, `TauCeti.posRootCone` and `TauCeti.dominantChamber` already do in
the sibling files `TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/RootSystem/Positive.lean` and
`TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/RootSystem/Chamber.lean`.

The translation part of an affine automorphism of a module is what makes the description of the
affine Weyl group work, and the two statements about it that the argument needs mention no root
pairing, so they live in `TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/AffineSpace/AffineEquiv.lean` beside Mathlib's
affine-equivalence API rather than here: `AffineEquiv.apply_eq_linear_add` and
`AffineEquiv.eq_constVAdd_mul_linear`.

## References

The construction is the one in N. Bourbaki, *Groupes et algèbres de Lie*, Chapters IV--VI, §2, and
in J. E. Humphreys, *Reflection Groups and Coxeter Groups*, Ch. 4. That the affine Weyl group of a
finite irreducible root system acts simply transitively on the alcoves is not proved here: that
statement needs the fundamental alcove, hence the highest root.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

open Set

universe u v w x

variable {ι : Type u} {R : Type v} {M : Type w} {N : Type x}
  [CommRing R] [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N]

variable (P : RootPairing ι R M N)

/-! ### Affine reflections -/

/-- The **affine reflection** of the weight space of a root pairing in the affine hyperplane
`{x | ⟨x, αᵢ^∨⟩ = k}`: the linear reflection in the `i`-th root, followed by the translation by
`k αᵢ`. -/
def affineReflection (i : ι) (k : R) : M ≃ᵃ[R] M :=
  (P.reflection i).toAffineEquiv.trans (AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M (k • P.root i))

@[simp]
theorem affineReflection_apply (i : ι) (k : R) (x : M) :
    affineReflection P i k x = x - (P.coroot' i x - k) • P.root i := by
  rw [affineReflection, AffineEquiv.coe_trans, Function.comp_apply, LinearEquiv.coe_toAffineEquiv,
    RootPairing.reflection_apply, AffineEquiv.constVAdd_apply, vadd_eq_add]
  module

/-- The linear part of an affine reflection is the linear reflection, whatever the level of the
wall. -/
@[simp]
theorem linear_affineReflection (i : ι) (k : R) :
    (affineReflection P i k).linear = P.reflection i :=
  (rfl)

/-- The affine reflection in the wall through the origin is the linear reflection. -/
theorem affineReflection_zero (i : ι) :
    affineReflection P i 0 = (P.reflection i).toAffineEquiv := by
  ext x
  rw [affineReflection_apply, LinearEquiv.coe_toAffineEquiv, RootPairing.reflection_apply, sub_zero]

/-- The value of an affine reflection at the origin is an integral multiple of its root as soon as
the level of its wall is an integer. This is why the affine Weyl group translates by the root
lattice. -/
theorem affineReflection_intCast_apply_zero (i : ι) (n : ℤ) :
    affineReflection P i (n : R) 0 = n • P.root i := by
  rw [affineReflection_apply, map_zero, ← Int.cast_smul_eq_zsmul R n (P.root i)]
  module

/-- An affine reflection fixes its wall pointwise. -/
theorem affineReflection_apply_of_coroot'_eq {i : ι} {k : R} {x : M} (h : P.coroot' i x = k) :
    affineReflection P i k x = x := by
  rw [affineReflection_apply, h, sub_self, zero_smul, sub_zero]

/-- An affine reflection reverses the coroot functional of its wall about the level of that
wall.

This is deliberately not a `simp` lemma: `TauCeti.affineReflection_apply` already rewrites the
argument of the coroot functional on the left-hand side, so the left-hand side is not in
`simp`-normal form. -/
theorem coroot'_affineReflection_apply (i : ι) (k : R) (x : M) :
    P.coroot' i (affineReflection P i k x) = 2 * k - P.coroot' i x := by
  rw [affineReflection_apply, map_sub, map_smul, RootPairing.root_coroot'_eq_pairing,
    P.pairing_same, smul_eq_mul]
  ring

/-- An affine reflection is an involution. -/
@[simp]
theorem affineReflection_mul_self (i : ι) (k : R) :
    affineReflection P i k * affineReflection P i k = 1 := by
  ext x
  simp only [AffineEquiv.coe_mul, Function.comp_apply, affineReflection_apply, map_sub, map_smul,
    RootPairing.root_coroot'_eq_pairing, P.pairing_same, smul_eq_mul, AffineEquiv.coe_one, id_eq]
  module

@[simp]
theorem affineReflection_inv (i : ι) (k : R) :
    (affineReflection P i k)⁻¹ = affineReflection P i k :=
  inv_eq_of_mul_eq_one_left (affineReflection_mul_self P i k)

/-- **Two affine reflections in parallel walls compose to a translation** by the corresponding
multiple of the root. This is the source of the translations in the affine Weyl group. -/
theorem affineReflection_mul_affineReflection (i : ι) (k l : R) :
    affineReflection P i k * affineReflection P i l
      = AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M ((k - l) • P.root i) := by
  ext x
  simp only [AffineEquiv.coe_mul, Function.comp_apply, affineReflection_apply, map_sub, map_smul,
    RootPairing.root_coroot'_eq_pairing, P.pairing_same, smul_eq_mul,
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd_apply, vadd_eq_add]
  module

/-- The translation by a root is the product of the affine reflections in the walls of that root at
levels `1` and `0`. -/
theorem constVAdd_root_eq_affineReflection_mul (i : ι) :
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M (P.root i)
      = affineReflection P i 1 * affineReflection P i 0 := by
  rw [affineReflection_mul_affineReflection, sub_zero, one_smul]

/-- **Conjugating an affine reflection by a translation shifts the level of its wall** by the coroot
value of the translation vector. -/
theorem constVAdd_mul_affineReflection_mul_constVAdd_inv (i : ι) (k : R) (v : M) :
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M v * affineReflection P i k * (AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M v)⁻¹
      = affineReflection P i (k + P.coroot' i v) := by
  ext x
  simp only [AffineEquiv.coe_mul, Function.comp_apply, AffineEquiv.inv_def,
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd_symm, AffineEquiv.constVAdd_apply, vadd_eq_add,
    affineReflection_apply, map_add, map_neg]
  module

/-- A linear reflection, read as an affine automorphism, is its own inverse. -/
@[simp]
theorem reflection_toAffineEquiv_inv (j : ι) :
    ((P.reflection j).toAffineEquiv)⁻¹ = (P.reflection j).toAffineEquiv := by
  rw [← affineReflection_zero, affineReflection_inv]

/-- **Conjugating an affine reflection by a linear reflection permutes the roots and keeps the
level of the wall.** With `TauCeti.constVAdd_mul_affineReflection_mul_constVAdd_inv` this says the
affine Weyl group permutes the affine walls among themselves. -/
theorem reflection_toAffineEquiv_mul_affineReflection_mul_inv (i j : ι) (k : R) :
    (P.reflection j).toAffineEquiv * affineReflection P i k
        * ((P.reflection j).toAffineEquiv)⁻¹
      = affineReflection P (P.reflectionPerm j i) k := by
  have hinv : ∀ y : M, P.reflection j (P.reflection j y) = y := fun y ↦ by
    have h : (P.reflection j).symm = P.reflection j := P.reflection_inv j
    rw [← h]
    exact (P.reflection j).symm_apply_apply y
  ext x
  simp only [reflection_toAffineEquiv_inv, AffineEquiv.coe_mul, Function.comp_apply,
    LinearEquiv.coe_toAffineEquiv, affineReflection_apply, map_sub, map_smul, hinv,
    RootPairing.coroot'_reflection, ← RootPairing.root_reflectionPerm]

/-- **The affine Weyl group is strictly larger than the Weyl group** as soon as the root pairing has
a root: it contains the translation by that root, and the translation by a nonzero vector moves the
origin, so it is not the affine automorphism underlying any linear one. -/
theorem constVAdd_root_ne_toAffineEquiv [NeZero (2 : R)] (i : ι) (w : M ≃ₗ[R] M) :
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M (P.root i) ≠ w.toAffineEquiv := by
  intro h
  refine P.ne_zero i ?_
  have hzero := congrFun (congrArg (fun f : M ≃ᵃ[R] M ↦ (f : M → M)) h) 0
  rwa [AffineEquiv.constVAdd_apply, vadd_eq_add, add_zero, LinearEquiv.coe_toAffineEquiv,
    map_zero] at hzero

/-! ### The Weyl group preserves the root lattice -/

/-- The subgroup of linear automorphisms of the weight space preserving the root lattice. -/
private def rootSpanStabilizer : Subgroup (M ≃ₗ[R] M) where
  carrier := {w | ∀ v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ, w v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ ∧ w.symm v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ}
  one_mem' _ hv := ⟨hv, hv⟩
  -- The two type ascriptions reduce the product and its inverse to iterated application, which
  -- is definitional for `LinearEquiv`: Mathlib supplies `LinearEquiv.mul_apply` but no lemma
  -- rewriting the group inverse to `LinearEquiv.symm`.
  mul_mem' {w w'} hw hw' v hv :=
    ⟨show w (w' v) ∈ _ from (hw (w' v) (hw' v hv).1).1,
      show w'.symm (w.symm v) ∈ _ from (hw' (w.symm v) (hw v hv).2).2⟩
  inv_mem' hw v hv := ⟨(hw v hv).2, (hw v hv).1⟩

private theorem reflection_mem_rootSpanStabilizer (i : ι) :
    P.reflection i ∈ rootSpanStabilizer P := by
  have key : ∀ v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ, P.reflection i v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ := by
    intro v hv
    induction hv using Submodule.span_induction with
    | mem v hv =>
      obtain ⟨j, rfl⟩ := hv
      rw [← RootPairing.root_reflectionPerm]
      exact Submodule.subset_span (mem_range_self _)
    | zero => simp
    | add u v _ _ hu hv => simpa using Submodule.add_mem _ hu hv
    | smul n v _ hv => simpa using Submodule.smul_mem _ n hv
  have hsymm : (P.reflection i).symm = P.reflection i := P.reflection_inv i
  exact fun v hv ↦ ⟨key v hv, hsymm ▸ key v hv⟩

/-- **The Weyl group preserves the root lattice**: a product of linear reflections carries an
integral combination of roots to an integral combination of roots. -/
theorem mem_rootSpan_of_mem_closure_range_reflection {w : M ≃ₗ[R] M}
    (hw : w ∈ Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection)) {v : M} (hv : v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ) :
    w v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ := by
  have hle : Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection) ≤ rootSpanStabilizer P := by
    rw [Subgroup.closure_le]
    rintro - ⟨i, rfl⟩
    exact reflection_mem_rootSpanStabilizer P i
  exact (hle hw v hv).1

/-! ### The affine Weyl group -/

/-- The **affine Weyl group** of a root pairing: the subgroup of affine automorphisms of the weight
space generated by the affine reflections in the walls at integral levels. -/
def affineWeylGroup : Subgroup (M ≃ᵃ[R] M) :=
  Subgroup.closure {f | ∃ (i : ι) (n : ℤ), f = affineReflection P i (n : R)}

theorem affineReflection_intCast_mem_affineWeylGroup (i : ι) (n : ℤ) :
    affineReflection P i (n : R) ∈ affineWeylGroup P := by
  rw [affineWeylGroup]
  exact Subgroup.subset_closure ⟨i, n, rfl⟩

/-- The linear reflections belong to the affine Weyl group. -/
theorem reflection_toAffineEquiv_mem_affineWeylGroup (i : ι) :
    (P.reflection i).toAffineEquiv ∈ affineWeylGroup P := by
  rw [← affineReflection_zero P i]
  simpa using affineReflection_intCast_mem_affineWeylGroup P i 0

/-- The translation by a root belongs to the affine Weyl group. -/
theorem constVAdd_root_mem_affineWeylGroup (i : ι) :
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M (P.root i) ∈ affineWeylGroup P := by
  rw [constVAdd_root_eq_affineReflection_mul P i]
  refine mul_mem ?_ ?_
  · simpa using affineReflection_intCast_mem_affineWeylGroup P i 1
  · simpa using affineReflection_intCast_mem_affineWeylGroup P i 0

/-- The translation by any element of the root lattice belongs to the affine Weyl group. -/
theorem constVAdd_mem_affineWeylGroup_of_mem_rootSpan {v : M} (hv : v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ) :
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M v ∈ affineWeylGroup P := by
  induction hv using Submodule.span_induction with
  | mem v hv =>
    obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hv
    exact constVAdd_root_mem_affineWeylGroup P i
  | zero =>
    rw [AffineEquiv.constVAdd_zero]
    exact one_mem (affineWeylGroup P)
  | add u v _ _ hu hv =>
    -- `AffineEquiv.constVAdd_add` states the composite as `AffineEquiv.trans`, which is the
    -- group multiplication of `M ≃ᵃ[R] M` with its arguments swapped.
    rw [AffineEquiv.constVAdd_add]
    exact mul_mem hu hv
  | smul n v _ hv =>
    rw [AffineEquiv.constVAdd_zsmul]
    exact zpow_mem hv n

/-- Every element of the Weyl group, read as an affine automorphism fixing the origin, belongs to
the affine Weyl group. -/
theorem toAffineEquiv_mem_affineWeylGroup {w : M ≃ₗ[R] M}
    (hw : w ∈ Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection)) :
    w.toAffineEquiv ∈ affineWeylGroup P := by
  induction hw using Subgroup.closure_induction with
  | mem w hw =>
    obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hw
    exact reflection_toAffineEquiv_mem_affineWeylGroup P i
  | one =>
    rw [LinearEquiv.toAffineEquiv_one]
    exact one_mem (affineWeylGroup P)
  | mul u v _ _ hu hv =>
    rw [LinearEquiv.toAffineEquiv_mul]
    exact mul_mem hu hv
  | inv u _ hu =>
    rw [LinearEquiv.toAffineEquiv_inv]
    exact inv_mem hu

/-- The affine automorphisms whose linear part is in the Weyl group and whose translation part is in
the root lattice. This is the affine Weyl group, by `TauCeti.mem_affineWeylGroup_iff`. -/
private def affineWeylCandidate : Subgroup (M ≃ᵃ[R] M) where
  carrier := {f | f.linear ∈ Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection) ∧ f 0 ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ}
  one_mem' := ⟨one_mem _, Submodule.zero_mem _⟩
  mul_mem' {f g} hf hg := by
    refine ⟨by rw [AffineEquiv.linear_mul]; exact mul_mem hf.1 hg.1, ?_⟩
    rw [AffineEquiv.coe_mul, Function.comp_apply, AffineEquiv.apply_eq_linear_add f (g 0)]
    exact Submodule.add_mem _
      (mem_rootSpan_of_mem_closure_range_reflection P hf.1 hg.2) hf.2
  inv_mem' {f} hf := by
    -- `hlin` is stated for the group inverse of the linear part of `f`, which is the linear part
    -- of `f⁻¹` and also `LinearEquiv.symm` of the linear part; both identifications are
    -- definitional and Mathlib names neither.
    have hlin : f.linear⁻¹ ∈ Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection) := inv_mem hf.1
    refine ⟨hlin, ?_⟩
    have h0 : f.linear (f⁻¹ 0) + f 0 = 0 := by
      rw [← AffineEquiv.apply_eq_linear_add f (f⁻¹ 0), AffineEquiv.inv_def]
      exact f.apply_symm_apply 0
    have hinv : f⁻¹ 0 = f.linear.symm (-f 0) := by
      rw [← LinearEquiv.symm_apply_apply f.linear (f⁻¹ 0)]
      exact congrArg f.linear.symm (by linear_combination (norm := abel) h0)
    rw [hinv]
    exact mem_rootSpan_of_mem_closure_range_reflection P
      hlin (Submodule.neg_mem _ hf.2)

/-- **The affine Weyl group is the semidirect product `Q ⋊ W` of the root lattice by the Weyl
group**: an affine automorphism of the weight space lies in the affine Weyl group exactly when its
linear part is a product of linear reflections and its value at the origin is an integral
combination of roots. -/
theorem mem_affineWeylGroup_iff {f : M ≃ᵃ[R] M} :
    f ∈ affineWeylGroup P ↔
      f.linear ∈ Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection) ∧ f 0 ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ := by
  refine ⟨fun hf ↦ ?_, fun ⟨hlin, h0⟩ ↦ ?_⟩
  · have hle : affineWeylGroup P ≤ affineWeylCandidate P := by
      rw [affineWeylGroup, Subgroup.closure_le]
      rintro - ⟨i, n, rfl⟩
      refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩
      · rw [linear_affineReflection]
        exact Subgroup.subset_closure (mem_range_self i)
      · rw [affineReflection_intCast_apply_zero]
        exact Submodule.smul_mem _ n (Submodule.subset_span (mem_range_self i))
    exact hle hf
  · rw [AffineEquiv.eq_constVAdd_mul_linear f]
    exact mul_mem (constVAdd_mem_affineWeylGroup_of_mem_rootSpan P h0)
      (toAffineEquiv_mem_affineWeylGroup P hlin)

/-- The factorisation of an element of the affine Weyl group into a translation by the root lattice
and an element of the Weyl group. -/
theorem eq_constVAdd_mul_of_mem_affineWeylGroup {f : M ≃ᵃ[R] M} (hf : f ∈ affineWeylGroup P) :
    ∃ v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ, ∃ w ∈ Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection),
      f = AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M v * w.toAffineEquiv := by
  rw [mem_affineWeylGroup_iff] at hf
  exact ⟨f 0, hf.2, f.linear, hf.1, AffineEquiv.eq_constVAdd_mul_linear f⟩

/-- **The translations in the affine Weyl group are exactly the translations by the root
lattice.** Together with `TauCeti.map_linearHom_affineWeylGroup` this is the kernel-image
description of the extension `Q ⋊ W`. -/
theorem constVAdd_mem_affineWeylGroup_iff (v : M) :
    AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M v ∈ affineWeylGroup P ↔ v ∈ P.rootSpan ℤ := by
  have h0 : AffineEquiv.constVAdd R M v 0 = v := by
    rw [AffineEquiv.constVAdd_apply, vadd_eq_add, add_zero]
  rw [mem_affineWeylGroup_iff, h0, AffineEquiv.linear_constVAdd]
  exact ⟨And.right, fun h ↦ ⟨one_mem _, h⟩⟩

/-- **The linear parts of the affine Weyl group are exactly the Weyl group.** -/
theorem map_linearHom_affineWeylGroup :
    (affineWeylGroup P).map AffineEquiv.linearHom = Subgroup.closure (range P.reflection) := by
  refine le_antisymm (fun w hw ↦ ?_) fun w hw ↦ ?_
  · obtain ⟨f, hf, rfl⟩ := hw
    exact (mem_affineWeylGroup_iff P |>.mp hf).1
  · refine ⟨w.toAffineEquiv, ?_, rfl⟩
    rw [SetLike.mem_coe, mem_affineWeylGroup_iff]
    refine ⟨AffineEquiv.linear_toAffineEquiv w ▸ hw, ?_⟩
    rw [LinearEquiv.coe_toAffineEquiv, map_zero]
    exact Submodule.zero_mem _

/-- The linear parts of the affine Weyl group, read through Mathlib's realisation of the Weyl group
as a subgroup of the automorphisms of the root pairing. -/
theorem map_linearHom_affineWeylGroup_eq_range_weightHom :
    (affineWeylGroup P).map AffineEquiv.linearHom =
      MonoidHom.range ((RootPairing.Equiv.weightHom P).domRestrict P.weylGroup) := by
  rw [map_linearHom_affineWeylGroup, P.range_weylGroup_weightHom]

/-- The semidirect-product description on the coweight space: the affine Weyl group of the flipped
pairing translates by the coroot lattice and reflects by the linear coreflections. This is the group
that acts on the space containing the fundamental alcove. -/
theorem mem_affineWeylGroup_flip_iff {f : N ≃ᵃ[R] N} :
    f ∈ affineWeylGroup P.flip ↔
      f.linear ∈ Subgroup.closure (range P.coreflection) ∧ f 0 ∈ P.corootSpan ℤ :=
  mem_affineWeylGroup_iff P.flip

end TauCeti
